(1)	A minimum tax trust is a trust estate that is none of the following:
(a)	a fixed trust (within the meaning of section 272-65 in Schedule 2F);
(b)	a special disability trust;
(c)	the trust estate of a deceased person;
(d)	a complying superannuation entity (within the meaning of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997);
(e)	a trust estate of a kind determined under subsection (2).
(2)	The Minister may, by legislative instrument, determine that trust estates of a specified kind are not minimum tax trusts if the Minister is satisfied that:
(a)	trust estates of that kind are of a similar nature to those covered by paragraph (1)(a), (b), (c) or (d) or by an earlier determination under this subsection; or
(b)	trust estates of that kind do not have a sufficient economic connection to Australia; or
(c)	trust estates of that kind, or their trustees, have statutory functions under an Australian law (within the meaning of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997).

(1)	A trust is a fixed trust if:
(a)	the trust’s beneficiaries have fixed entitlements to all of the income and capital of the trust; or
(b)	there are no material discretionary elements affecting the entitlements or rights of the trust’s beneficiaries.
(2)	For the purposes of paragraph (1)(b), matters suggesting there are no such material discretionary elements include the following:
(a)	the beneficiaries have clearly defined specific and enforceable entitlements or rights:
(i)	to all of the income and capital of the trust; or
(ii)	in relation to the governance of the trust;
whether or not the entitlements or rights are subject to rules, so long as none of the rules are discretionary;
(b)	if there are powers to do any of the following:
(i)	vary such entitlements or rights;
(ii)	issue new entitlements or rights for one or more beneficiaries;
(iii)	issue new interests in the trust;
(iv)	classify payments received by the trust as income or capital;
(v)	deal with administrative matters;
the powers cannot be exercised to significantly vary existing entitlements or rights, or to significantly affect the value of the interests of existing beneficiaries of the trust;
(c)	a power to vary the instrument establishing the trust that can only be exercised:
(i)	through a process requiring the consent of all beneficiaries; or
(ii)	in a way that cannot adversely affect the entitlements or rights of beneficiaries;
(d)	a matter determined under paragraph (4)(a).
Note:	It follows that the existence of anything contrary to any of the above paragraphs suggests that the trust is not a fixed trust.
(3)	For the purposes of paragraph (1)(b) and despite subsection (2), if a matter determined under paragraph (4)(b) applies to a trust then there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the entitlements or rights of the trust’s beneficiaries.
(4)	The Minister may by legislative instrument:
(a)	determine matters that suggest there are no material discretionary elements affecting the entitlements or rights of the beneficiaries of a specified kind of trust; or
(b)	determine matters that mean there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the entitlements or rights of the beneficiaries of a specified kind of trust.

(1)	A company is an eligible company if there are no material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the company’s members.
(2)	For the purposes of subsection (1), matters suggesting there are no such material discretionary elements include the following:
(a)	the members have clearly defined, specific and enforceable rights and interests:
(i)	to all of the income and capital of the company; or
(ii)	in relation to the governance of the company;
whether or not the rights or interests are subject to rules, so long as none of the rules are discretionary;
(b)	if there are powers to do any of the following:
(i)	vary such rights or interests;
(ii)	issue new rights or interests for one or more members;
(iii)	issue new rights or interests in the company;
(iv)	deal with administrative matters;
the powers cannot be exercised to significantly vary existing rights or interests, or to significantly affect the value of the rights or interests of existing members of the company;
(c)	a power to vary the constituent documents of the company that can only be exercised:
(i)	through a process requiring the consent of all members; or
(ii)	in a way that cannot adversely affect the rights or interests of members;
(d)	a matter determined under paragraph (4)(a).
(3)	For the purposes of subsection (1) and despite subsection (2), if a matter determined under paragraph (4)(b) applies to a company then there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the company’s members.
(4)	The Minister may by legislative instrument:
(a)	determine matters that suggest there are no material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the members of a specified kind of company; or
(b)	determine matters that mean there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the members of a specified kind of company.

(1)	This section sets out the rate of tax payable by a trustee under section 101AA of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 in respect of so much of the net income of the trust estate for a year of income as is minimum tax income.
(2)	The rate is as follows:
(a)	to the extent that, as a result of one or more other provisions of this Act, the tax payable on that minimum tax income is payable at a rate that falls short of 30%—a rate equal to that shortfall;
(b)	to the extent that, as a result of one or more other provisions of this Act, the tax payable on that minimum tax income is payable at a rate that is equal to or exceeds 30%—nil;
(c)	to the extent that no tax is payable on that minimum tax income as a result of any other provision of this Act—30%.
Example:	For paragraphs (a) and (b), the rate at which tax is payable as a result of subsection 12(6) could be a composite rate resulting from the operation of more than one provision in section 13, 14 or 15 or Schedule 10 or 12.

(1)	A roll-over under this Subdivision is available in relation to an asset that, under a transaction, the trustee of a minimum tax trust (the transferor) transfers to another entity (the transferee) if:
(a)	the transaction is, or is part of, a restructure of the minimum tax trust that transfers all of the relevant trust assets out of that discretion-based trust structure; and
(b)	the transfer of the asset under the transaction occurs during the period starting on 1 July 2027 and ending on 30 June 2030 (the transitional relief period); and
(c)	the transferor and the transferee chose, in accordance with section 126-431, to apply a roll-over under this Subdivision in relation to the assets transferred under the transaction; and
(d)	the notification requirement in section 126-432 is satisfied (if applicable) in relation to the income year in which the asset is transferred; and
(e)	the continuity requirements in section 126-440 are satisfied in relation to the transaction; and
(f)	the transferor and the transferee meet the residency requirement in section 126-445 for an entity.
(2)	However, a roll-over under this Subdivision is not available if the transferee is an exempt entity, a complying superannuation entity or a trustee of a minimum tax trust.
No roll-over relief available unless all relevant assets transferred
(3)	Despite subsection (1), a roll-over is not available under this Subdivision in relation to the asset if, at the end of the transitional relief period, the transferor has not transferred to the transferee all of the assets held by the transferor in the transferor’s capacity as trustee of the minimum tax trust (excluding any assets referred to in subsection (4)).
(4)	The following assets are excluded from the requirement in subsection (3):
(a)	any asset not capable of being transferred;
(b)	any CGT asset used to generate assessable income from carrying on a primary production business;
(c)	any asset reasonably required to discharge liabilities properly incurred by the transferor as trustee of the minimum tax trust, including tax liabilities and amounts payable to the transferor under rights of indemnity or reimbursement;
(d)	if the minimum tax trust is intended to be wound up as a result of the restructure—any asset reasonably required to pay such costs and expenses as part of the costs of the winding up;
(e)	any asset that cost $1,000 or less at the time it was acquired by the minimum tax trust.
No roll-over relief available if discretionary outcomes retained
(5)	Despite subsection (1), a roll-over is not available under this Subdivision in relation to the asset if, during the period:
(a)	starting on the day of the last transfer of an asset of the trust required to avoid a roll-over becoming unavailable because of subsection (3); and
(b)	ending at the end of 4 income years after the income year in which that last transfer occurs;
there are material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of members of the transferee entity.
(6)	For the purposes of subsection (5), matters suggesting there are no material discretionary elements include the following:
(a)	the members of the entity have clearly defined specific and enforceable rights or interests:
(i)	to all of the income and capital of the entity; or
(ii)	in relation to the governance of the entity;
whether or not the rights or interests are subject to rules, so long as none of the rules are discretionary;
(b)	if there are powers to do any of the following:
(i)	vary such rights or interests;
(ii)	issue new rights or interests for one or more members;
(iii)	issue new rights or interests in the entity;
(iv)	deal with administrative matters;
the powers cannot be exercised to significantly vary existing rights or interests, or to significantly affect the value of the rights or interests of existing members of the entity;
(c)	a power to vary the entity’s constituent documents establishing the entity that can only be exercised:
(i)	through a process requiring the consent of all members; or
(ii)	in a way that cannot adversely affect the rights or interests of members;
(d)	a matter determined under paragraph (8)(a).
(7)	For the purposes of subsection (5) and despite subsection (6), if a matter determined under paragraph (8)(b) applies to an entity then there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the entity’s members.
(8)	The Minister may, by legislative instrument:
(a)	determine matters that suggest there are no material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the members of a specified kind of entity; or
(b)	determine matters that mean there are one or more material discretionary elements affecting the rights or interests of the members of a specified kind of entity.
Extended period for amendment of assessments
(9)	Section 170 of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 does not prevent the amendment of an assessment of an entity by the Commissioner if all of the following apply:
(a)	the assessment is of:
(i)	the transferor; or
(ii)	a beneficiary of the minimum tax trust; or
(iii)	the transferee; or
(iv)	a member of the transferee;
(b)	the assessment is for an income year in which an asset is transferred under a transaction to which a choice by the transferor and transferee under paragraph (1)(c) applies;
(c)	the Commissioner amends the assessment within 4 years after the day on which the Commissioner gives notice of the assessment to the entity;
(d)	the amendment of the assessment is connected to the availability or otherwise of a roll-over under this Subdivision in relation to the asset.

(1)	The income tax law applies to an entity in relation to the transfer of an asset by the entity, or to the entity, as if the transfer takes place for the asset’s roll-over cost if:
(a)	the transfer occurs under a transaction in relation to which section 126-430 applies; and
(b)	a roll-over under this Subdivision is available under that section in relation to the asset.
(2)	The asset’s roll-over cost is whichever of the following amounts is applicable in relation to the transfer:
(a)	in relation to the application of subsection (1) to the asset as a CGT asset (other than trading stock, a revenue asset or a depreciating asset)—the transferor’s cost base for the asset just before the transfer takes effect;
(b)	in relation to the application of subsection (1) to the asset as trading stock—the amount equal to:
(i)	the cost of the item for the transferor; or
(ii)	if the transferor held the item as trading stock at the start of the income year—the value of the item for the transferor then;
(c)	in relation to the application of subsection (1) to the asset as a revenue asset—the amount that would give rise to the transferor not making a profit or a loss on the transfer;
(d)	in relation to the application of subsection (1) to the asset as a depreciating asset—the adjustable value of the asset just before the transfer takes effect.
